---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Us | ChinaStyle Traditional Wear Guide"
  description="Learn about ChinaStyle — your guide to Chinese traditional clothing experiences. From Hanfu to Qipao, we help travelers discover authentic fashion."
  canonicalUrl="/about"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Home", url: "/" }, { name: "About", url: "/about/" }]}>
  <section class="pt-24 pb-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <span class="text-xs font-medium text-[#b5343a] tracking-[0.15em] uppercase">About</span>
      <h1 class="font-serif-sc text-3xl sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-bold leading-tight tracking-tight text-stone-900 mt-3">
        About ChinaStyle
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-stone-500 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">
        ChinaStyle is an independent travel guide dedicated to one thing: helping you experience Chinese traditional clothing in the places that shaped it.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">Our Mission</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">Chinese traditional wear — Hanfu, qipao, Tang suits, Zhongshan suits, and the textile traditions of China''s 55 ethnic minorities — represents thousands of years of craftsmanship, culture, and identity. But most visitors to China never get past looking at museum displays. We believe the best way to understand these traditions is to wear them, in the cities and villages where they were born. Whether that means a Hanfu photoshoot on Xi''an''s ancient city walls, a custom qipao fitting in a Shanghai lane house, or a Miao silver-smithing workshop in a Guizhou mountain village — we show you where to go, what to expect, and how to make it happen.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">What We Cover</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">Every guide on ChinaStyle is researched firsthand, with practical details you won''t find in generic travel guides: exact rental shop prices in RMB, turnaround times for custom tailoring, which apps to use for booking, what to wear underneath, and how to navigate language barriers. We cover Hanfu experiences in Xi''an and Luoyang, qipao tailoring in Shanghai, Tang suit workshops in Beijing, Zhongshan suit heritage tailors in Nanjing, ethnic minority textile villages in Guizhou and Yunnan, and the practical travel logistics — visas, transport, seasons, and budgets — that make it all possible.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">Our Approach</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">We write for travelers who value authentic experiences over checklists. Our recommendations prioritize independent tailors, family-run workshops, and community-based tourism initiatives over mass-market operators. We are not affiliated with any rental shop, tailor, or tour company. If we recommend a place, it''s because we genuinely believe it offers the best experience for your time and money.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">Contact</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">Have a question about planning your Chinese traditional clothing experience? Found a great tailor or rental shop we should know about? We welcome reader tips, corrections, and suggestions.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>